title op6: a rename of one name against a split

# The case set-valued succession keeps exactly as it was, included
# because a settlement that changes everything it touches is not a
# settlement, it is a rewrite.
#
# Onto un-shares z into its own dnode.  Off-of renames one name, x to
# aa.  The delta is lost {x}, gained {aa}.
#
# With one lost and one gained the pairing is forced BY THE SETS
# THEMSELVES -- there is nothing to recognise and nothing to guess --
# so the rename routes to the fragment holding x.  That was true
# under Definition 9.3's antecedent machinery and it is true under
# deltas; the two rules agree here and the reasoning gets shorter.
#
# Realization never reaches the fallback.  Pass one is name-driven
# and the output pool holding aa also holds y, a name onto still has
# on pool 10, so y resolves and claims that dnode.  The split
# fragment's z resolves to 30-200 the same way.
#
# WORKED OUTCOME (by hand, before any implementation ran)
#   believed moves:  none; no directory changed
#   deltas:          pool 10 lost {x}, gained {aa}; z is not in the
#                    delta, it is onto's split
#   decisions:       output pool {aa, y} reuses onto's 10-100 by the
#                    surviving name y; output pool {z} reuses 30-200
#   conflicts:       none
#   emission:        one rename, x to aa, inside /shared's scope
#
# The corpus already holds this shape as the one-rename/ arm of
# 6-ambiguous-succession.tree.  It is repeated alone so a change to
# it cannot hide inside a fixture that also covers something else.

tree base
  1-1: /
    2-100: shared/
      10-100: x = v0
      10-100: y
      10-100: z

tree onto
  1-1: /
    2-100: shared/
      10-100: x = v0
      10-100: y
      30-200: z = v0

tree offof
  1-1: /
    2-100: shared/
      10-100: aa = v0
      10-100: y
      10-100: z

# --- expected result, worked by hand 2026-08-31 ---
expect clean

tree expected
  1-1: /
    2-100: shared
      10-100: aa = v0
      10-100: y
      30-200: z = v0
